C adjacency lists: inserting an undirected edge without half-updates

## Why the old reference was quarantined

The previous reference pushed the forward edge `u -> v` into `head[u]` and only then allocated the reverse edge `v -> u`. If the second `malloc` failed, the function returned failure but the graph already contained a one-sided edge. Callers that trust the return value would believe nothing changed, while traversals from `u` see `v` and traversals from `v` do not see `u`. Undirected algorithms such as connected components or bipartite checks then give wrong answers.

## The rule: allocate everything, then publish

An operation that can fail should either complete fully or leave the structure exactly as it was (the "strong guarantee"). For an undirected edge, that means:

1. Validate the inputs (graph pointer, both vertex indices in range).
2. Allocate every node the operation needs.
3. If any allocation failed, free what was allocated and return an error. Nothing has been linked yet, so the graph is unchanged.
4. Only then link the nodes into the lists. Linking cannot fail.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct AdjEdge { size_t to; struct AdjEdge *next; } AdjEdge;
typedef struct { size_t n; AdjEdge **head; } AdjList;

enum { AL_OK = 0, AL_EINVAL = -1, AL_ENOMEM = -2 };

/* On any failure the graph is unchanged. */
int al_add_edge(AdjList *g, size_t u, size_t v, int directed) {
    if (g == NULL || u >= g->n || v >= g->n) return AL_EINVAL;
    int need_rev = !directed && u != v;
    AdjEdge *fwd = malloc(sizeof *fwd);
    AdjEdge *rev = need_rev ? malloc(sizeof *rev) : NULL;
    if (fwd == NULL || (need_rev && rev == NULL)) {
        free(fwd);           /* free(NULL) is a no-op */
        free(rev);
        return AL_ENOMEM;
    }
    fwd->to = v; fwd->next = g->head[u]; g->head[u] = fwd;
    if (need_rev) { rev->to = u; rev->next = g->head[v]; g->head[v] = rev; }
    return AL_OK;
}
```

Notes on this design:

- A self-loop `u == v` in an undirected graph is stored once here. Some definitions count it twice toward the degree; state the convention the rest of the code uses.
- Pushing at the head is O(1) and yields neighbours newest first. Algorithms that need a particular neighbour order must sort or append instead.
- Duplicate edges are allowed. If the graph must be simple, check for an existing edge first, which costs O(degree(u)).
- Using `size_t` for vertex indices avoids the `int` casts and negative-index checks of the old version.

## Matching operations

- Removing an undirected edge must remove both directions. If one direction is missing, report an inconsistency rather than silently succeeding.
- Freeing the graph walks every list, saving `next` before `free`ing each node, then frees `head` and resets fields so a second free is harmless.
- Creating the graph with `calloc(n, sizeof *g->head)` gives null heads. Most libraries, and C23, return NULL when `n * sizeof *head` overflows; for C11/C17 portability, check `n > SIZE_MAX / sizeof *g->head` first (`SIZE_MAX` is in `<stdint.h>`).

## Alternative: compressed sparse row (CSR)

For a static graph, store all neighbours in one array `adj` with an offset array `start` of size `n + 1`; vertex `u`'s neighbours are `adj[start[u]]` through `adj[start[u+1] - 1]`. Building CSR needs two passes (count degrees, then fill), allocates two arrays instead of one node per edge, and has better locality. It does not support cheap insertion.

## Status

This code was written for this reference. On 2026-10-18 it was compiled with GCC (`-std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=address,undefined`) and run against a small fault-injection harness that made the first or second `malloc` fail; in both cases the function returned `AL_ENOMEM` with both lists unchanged and no leaks reported. That is a smoke test, not a review. Keep `verified: false` until a reviewer adds it to the project's tests.

